{"id":"h284","run":2,"role":"calibration","category":"science-chemistry","question":"Two 50 kg pieces of copper, one at 175°C and the other at 40°C, are in contact with each other. The heat flow between the two pieces of copper is given by q =mC_P\\DeltaT, where C_P = 385 J kg^-1 K^-1 . Calculate the final tempera-ture and the amount of heat transferred between the two pieces of copper.\nIs the correct answer \"120°C and -1000 kJ\"?","truth":"no","model_answer":"no","correct":true,"source":"TIGER-Lab/MMLU-Pro, test split","claims":[{"text":"The proposed answer of 120°C and -1000 kJ is incorrect.","confidence":0.97,"lean":-1,"theta":1.5237,"phi":-3.1416},{"text":"With equal masses and equal specific heats, the final temperature is the average of the initial temperatures.","confidence":0.95,"lean":-1,"theta":1.4923,"phi":-3.1416},{"text":"The final temperature is 107.5°C, assuming no heat loss to the surroundings.","confidence":0.95,"lean":-1,"theta":1.4923,"phi":-3.1416},{"text":"The hot piece releases about 1300 kJ in cooling from 175°C to 107.5°C.","confidence":0.93,"lean":-1,"theta":1.4608,"phi":-3.1416},{"text":"The cold piece gains the same amount of heat that the hot piece loses.","confidence":0.93,"lean":-1,"theta":1.4608,"phi":-3.1416},{"text":"The magnitude of the heat transferred is about 1300 kJ, not 1000 kJ.","confidence":0.93,"lean":-1,"theta":1.4608,"phi":-3.1416},{"text":"A final temperature of 120°C would only follow if masses or heat capacities were unequal, which they are not.","confidence":0.85,"lean":-1,"theta":1.3352,"phi":-3.1416},{"text":"Heat lost by the hot piece is taken as negative, giving about -1300 kJ for that piece.","confidence":0.8,"lean":-1,"theta":1.2566,"phi":-3.1416}],"relations":[{"i":0,"j":1,"J":0.6,"relation":"supports"},{"i":0,"j":2,"J":0.9,"relation":"supports"},{"i":0,"j":3,"J":0.7,"relation":"supports"},{"i":0,"j":5,"J":0.8,"relation":"supports"},{"i":0,"j":6,"J":0.8,"relation":"supports"},{"i":1,"j":2,"J":0.9,"relation":"s